% ============================================================================
% superlative-adjective-rule — common English superlative-adjective
% formation rules and what each one actually requires (adj-facts-stdlib,
% LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% Turning an adjective into its superlative form ("the biggest", "the
% happiest") is governed by a small set of named rules, each covering a
% different spelling pattern the base adjective falls into. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? superlative_adjective_rule(adjective_ending_in_y, $D)
%       % change_y_to_i_before_est
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `superlative_adjective_rule(rule, description)` carrying the
% source citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query —
% the engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with
% zero model calls, and abstains on any rule not one of these three
% source-cited examples. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description
% and recall which rule it names:
%
%     ? superlative_adjective_rule($R, add_est_suffix)
%       % one_syllable_adjective
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "What Are Superlative
% Adjectives? Definition and Examples" article, which states each rule
% directly:
%
%     rule (this table's atom label)             description (this table's atom label)      rule quote (verbatim)
%     -------------------------------------------  ------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     one_syllable_adjective                        add_est_suffix                              "If an adjective has only one syllable, most of the time you can simply add
%                                                                                                  the suffix –est to the end of the word without changing the spelling."
%     one_syllable_consonant_vowel_consonant        double_final_consonant_before_est            "If the last three letters of a one-syllable adjective follow a
%                                                                                                  consonant-vowel-consonant format, like big or thin, you have to double the
%                                                                                                  last consonant and then add –est."
%     adjective_ending_in_y                         change_y_to_i_before_est                     "To make the superlative, first change the y into an i and then add –est."
%
% WebFetch-verified before writing (twice, across two cycles of this loop —
% the second pass specifically re-confirmed exact wording after an initial
% fetch surfaced a bullet-list fragment for a DIFFERENT, ultimately
% rejected rule about long adjectives). `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a
% reputable, widely-cited grammar-content company, the same tier this
% stdlib already reserves for other non-.gov language sources and the same
% source family already cited by `sentence-type.adj`/`part-of-speech.adj`/
% `contraction.adj`/`possessive-noun.adj`/`simile-meaning.adj`/
% `prefix-meaning.adj`/`capitalization-rule.adj`. A rule not one of these
% three — `three_or_more_syllable_adjective`, a real rule the same article
% covers (longer adjectives use "most" instead of "-est"), but whose own
% supporting text on the page is a bullet-list fragment rather than a
% clean quotable sentence, and which is not one of this table's three rows
% — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a description. (See ../README.md;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table superlative_adjective_rule {
    columns rule, description

    row (one_syllable_adjective, add_est_suffix)
    row (one_syllable_consonant_vowel_consonant, double_final_consonant_before_est)
    row (adjective_ending_in_y, change_y_to_i_before_est)

    source "If an adjective has only one syllable, most of the time you can simply add the suffix –est to the end of the word without changing the spelling."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/superlative-adjectives/"
    trust consensus
}
